semi-primes, while it is useless when $\bitsize{p_i}= \bitsize{q_i}$.

In 2011, Sarkar and Maitra \cite{sarkar2011} considered again the IFP 
of two semi\-primes $N_1$ and $N_2$ such that $\MSB{p_1}{t}=\MSB{p_2}{t}$.
They observed that the bit length of
$x_0=p_1-p_2$ is less than $\bitsize{p_i}$, and that
$\gcd(N_1,N_2+x_0\,q_2)=p_1$. Because $N_2$ can be regarded as an approximation
of $N_2+x_0\,q_2$, finding the greatest common divisor can be done efficiently
when some conditions are satisfied, as described in \cite{howgrave2001}. In
particular, $p_1$ can be recovered in polynomial time in $n$ if $t>
2\alpha-\alpha^2/n$.  Additionally, when $p_1>\abs{x_0\,q_2}$, either
$\left\lfloor N_2/p_1\right\rfloor$ or $\left\lceil N_2/p_1\right\rceil$ is
equal to $q_2$, hence $N_2$ can be factored too. Once again, this method does
not work for balanced semi-primes; actually, the theoretical bound on $t$ is
worse than the one in \cite{sarkar2009} when $\alpha>0.33\,n$. The authors also
extended their result to $k>2$ semi-primes with common $\MSB{p_i}{t}$,
however the algorithm is exponential in $k$, thus it is polynomial
only if $k=O(\log n)$.
 
In 2013, Kurosawa and Ueda \cite{kurosawa2013} improved the analysis of the
algorithm in \cite{may2009}, so that the bound on the number $t$ of shared less
significant bits is lowered from $t\geq 2\alpha+3$ to $t\geq 2\alpha+1$.
Interestingly, the authors also showed that if $\cmod{p_1}{p_2}{T}$ for some
$T>2^{2\alpha+1}$, then the semi-primes can be factored in time
$O(\log^2(\min\set{N_1,N_2}))$. Of course, this only applies to imbalanced
semi-primes.

In 2013, Lu, Zhang, and Lin \cite{lu2013} improved the algorithm in
\cite{sarkar2011} based on the approximate common divisor problem and get
a better bound on $t$ for shared LSB, shared MSB, and shared LSB/MSB when the number of
semi-primes $k$ is greater than three: $t/n\geq 1-(1-\alpha/n)^{k/(k-1)}+\epsilon$,
where $\epsilon$ is a term related to the exponential cost of the running
time in $k$: $(e/\epsilon)^k$. Once again, this method is not useful when applied
to balanced semi-primes.

In 2014, Peng, Hu, Xu, Huang, and Xie \cite{peng2014} reconsidered the case
$\LSB{p_1}{t}=\LSB{p_2}{t}$ with a new approach, the ``two-step lattice-based
method'': get a lower bound on $t$ such that the shared bits in $p_i$'s yield a
low-dimensional lattice including the target vector containing the unknown
factors.  Then, the target vector is expressed as an integer linear combination
of reduced basis of the lattice (computed by LLL algorithm). After some
transformations, the problem is reduced to a modular equation with unknown
modulus by means of the Chinese remainder theorem.  Finally, this equation can
be solved by a method of Hermann and May \cite{hermann2008}.  The bound for
$k=2$ semi-primes is $t\geq 2\alpha-\frac{3}{2}\frac{\alpha^2}{n}
-\frac{1}{4}\frac{\alpha^3}{n^2}+O(\frac{\alpha^4}{n^3})$. Noteworthy, the
result holds also for any $k>2$, and the corresponding lower bound for $t>2$
and large enough values of $k$ could be lower than $\alpha$; in these cases,
balanced semi-primes where $\bitsize{p_i}=\bitsize{q_i}=\alpha$ could be
theoretically factored. However, as $k$ increases, the lattice dimension
grows so much that the problem on balanced semi-primes remains computationally
infeasible.  In the same article, the authors applied the new idea also to the
case $\MSB{p_1}{t}=\MSB{p_2}{t}$ obtaining the same bounds as above.
It should be stressed that while the theoretical lower bounds are better than
those in the previous articles, the experimental results shows that the method
is from 100 to 1000 times slower than the older ones. 


