In 2014, Nitaj and Ariffin \cite{nitaj2015} proposed a method based
on the continued fraction algorithm to solve the IFP for two imbalanced semi-primes
$N_1=p_1\,q_1$ and $N_2=p_2\,q_2$ where the implicit information is that there
exist some unknown multiples $a_1\,p_1$ and $a_2\,p_2$ that share $t$ less
significant bits or $t$ most significant bits. In particular, for both the
MSB case and the LSB case the bound on $t$ is essentially $t\geq 2\alpha+2\beta+1$
where $\log_2 a_i\leq \beta$ (for slightly better bounds on the two separate
cases refer to the article). The authors extended also the method to $k>2$
semi-primes using simultaneous Diophantine approximations and lattice reduction;
they obtained as bound $t>(\alpha k+\beta k^2+k\,(1+\log_2(\pi\,e))/2)/(k-1)$.
In this algorithm the involved semi-primes may be of different sizes, however
the difference in the bit lengths of $p_i$ and $q_i$ is worse than in the previous
algorithms, because $\bitsize{p_i}-\bitsize{q_i}>\bitsize{q_i}+2\beta$.

In 2015, Nuida, Itakura, and Kurosawa \cite{nuida2015} improved the result in
\cite{kurosawa2013} concerning the implicit hint $\cmod{p_1}{p_2}{T}$, where
$T>2^{2\alpha+1}$. The algorithm proposed in this work refines the analysis in
\cite{may2009} and assumes that $T=Q^2+2^{O(\log\kappa)}$, where
$Q\geq\max\set{q_1,q_2}$ and $\kappa$ is a parameter affecting the running
time: specifically, the algorithm is polynomial in $n$ and $\kappa$. If $T=2^t$
and $\bitsize{Q}=\alpha$, then the bound becomes $t\geq 2\alpha-O(\log\kappa)$.
Observe that the method is effective only for highly imbalanced semi-primes,
because the running time is exponential in $\log\kappa$.

In 2015, Lu, Peng, Zhang, Hu, and Lin \cite{lu2016} improved the schema for
shared LSBs, shared MSBs, and shared LSBs plus MSBs adopted in
\cite{sarkar2011,lu2013}, and got an improved lower bound for the general case
$k\geq 2$.  Specifically, for $k=2$ the new lower bound is $t\geq
2\alpha-2\alpha^2/n$.  The authors also revisited the algorithm in
\cite{peng2014} and improved the corresponding lower bound.  Surprisingly,
this improved lower bound turns out to be identical to the one obtained by
